        <div class="cav">The scary number</div>
        <h2 id="myth">Is it true that an ATS auto-rejects 75 percent of resumes?</h2>
        <p>No. The widely repeated claim that applicant tracking systems automatically reject 75 percent of resumes before a human sees them is unfounded. It traces back to a resume-software vendor called Preptel around 2012, which shut down in 2013 and never published a study or a method behind the figure, which is why it also circulates as 70 and 88 percent (<a href="https://unchartedcareer.com/blog/the-75-of-resumes-are-auto-rejected-myth-traced-to-its-source" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Uncharted Career</a>). A 2025 study of 25 US recruiters found only 8 percent enable content-based auto-rejection at all, with 92 percent relying on human review after an initial filter (<a href="https://itbrief.co.uk/story/study-reveals-ats-rarely-auto-rejects-cvs-debunks-75-myth" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IT Brief, reporting Enhancv, 2025</a>). The real, sourced problem is different, and it is a human one.</p>
        <blockquote>88% of employers agree that qualified high-skills candidates are vetted out of the process <span class="accent">because they do not match the exact criteria established by the job description.</span><cite>Harvard Business School and Accenture, Hidden Workers, 2021</cite></blockquote>
        <p>That figure rises to 94 percent for middle-skills roles (<a href="https://www.hbs.edu/ris/Publication%20Files/hiddenworkers09032021_Fuller_white_paper_33a2047f-41dd-47b1-9a8d-bd08cf3bfa94.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Harvard Business School and Accenture, 2021</a>). Notice the wording: employers configure rigid, exact-match criteria, and good candidates are filtered because their resume does not use the exact terms. That is not a robot deleting resumes at random. It is a search that misses you when your resume does not speak the posting's language, which is precisely what tailoring fixes.</p>

        <div class="cav">The useful part</div>
        <h2 id="keywords">Which keywords should you match?</h2>
        <p>Match the keywords a recruiter is most likely to search for, in priority order: the skills and tools listed under required qualifications, the terms that appear more than once in the posting, and the exact job title and named certifications. Recruiters filter candidates in the system by skills far more than anything else, with skills used by 76.4 percent, education by 59.7 percent, job title by 55.3 percent, and certifications by 50.6 percent (<a href="https://www.jobscan.co/blog/tailor-resume-job-description/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jobscan, 2026</a>). Use the exact wording from the posting where it genuinely applies, since many systems favor exact matches over synonyms, and include both an abbreviation and its full form. The Best Answer Hub Job Keyword Matcher surfaces the high-frequency terms and shows which ones your resume is missing so you know where to focus.</p>
