        <div class="callout sage"><div class="h">Tailor honestly</div><p>Only add a keyword you can genuinely back up. The goal is to be found for the skills you actually have, not to claim ones you do not. Adding real, missing terms to your summary, skills section, and experience bullets is the whole job, and it is what the matcher is built to help you do.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The tempting shortcut</div>
        <h2 id="stuffing">Does keyword stuffing work?</h2>
        <p>No. Padding a resume with repeated keywords, or hiding them in white text, is easy to spot and it backfires. Jobscan calls hidden-text keyword stuffing "one of the quickest ways to burn a bridge with a recruiter," because the trick becomes plainly visible the moment the resume is parsed into the system, and resumes with unnatural keyword repetition rank lower on the recruiter-facing screens (<a href="https://www.jobscan.co/blog/resume-keyword-stuffing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jobscan</a>). The point of keyword matching is not to trick a machine. It is to make sure the real skills you have are present in the words a recruiter will search for, so include each important term naturally a couple of times rather than stacking it.</p>

        <div class="cav">The format trap</div>
        <h2 id="format">What resume formatting can break an ATS?</h2>
        <p>The formatting that breaks an ATS is anything the parser cannot read cleanly: images, charts, tables, columns, text boxes, and details buried in the header or footer. University career centers are consistent on this. The University of Virginia advises to "avoid images, charts, tables, and graphics" because "these are difficult for ATS to read," and to keep your name and contact details out of the header or footer (<a href="https://career.virginia.edu/Students/Prepare/Resumes/NavigatingATS" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UVA Career Center</a>). The University of Minnesota Duluth adds that standard headings like Education, Experience, and Technical Skills help the system sort your information correctly (<a href="https://career.d.umn.edu/students/resume-cover-letter/applicant-tracking-system-ats-tips" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UMN Duluth</a>). A skill that lands in a graphic the parser skips is a skill the recruiter never finds.</p>

        <div class="cav">The part that matters</div>
        <h2 id="privacy">Is it safe to upload your resume to a free ATS checker?</h2>
        <p>Uploading is the quiet risk, because a resume is a packet of personal data: your name, address, phone number, email, and full work history. Most free checkers process it on their servers and store it under their own privacy policies. Jobscan runs behind an account and its free tier caps you at five scans a month before a paywall of about 50 dollars (<a href="https://support.jobscan.co/hc/en-us/articles/360056018654-When-do-I-get-my-free-monthly-scans" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Jobscan Support</a>). Teal limits the free match to the top five keywords and SkillSyncer to one scan a week, both behind a login (<a href="https://www.tealhq.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Teal</a>, <a href="https://skillsyncer.com/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SkillSyncer</a>). The Best Answer Hub Job Keyword Matcher takes the opposite approach: it compares the two texts in your browser, so your resume never reaches a server, which you can confirm by going offline after the page loads.</p>
        <div class="callout peach"><div class="h">Check it in ten seconds</div><p>Open your browser developer tools, switch to the Network tab, and run a match. If your resume truly stays on your device, you will see no text being uploaded. Do this once with the Best Answer Hub Job Keyword Matcher and you can confirm nothing leaves your machine.</p></div>

        <div class="cav">The honest comparison</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is it different from Jobscan?</h2>
        <p>The difference is that the Best Answer Hub Job Keyword Matcher is free and unlimited, needs no account, and keeps your resume in your browser, where the big scanners cap the free tier, require a login, and upload the resume to their servers. It is also honest about what an ATS does, rather than leaning on the "auto-rejected" fear that sells subscriptions. The trade-off is that it does not simulate a specific company's ATS scoring; it finds the missing keywords, which is the core need for most applicants. The table sets the usual experience next to this one.</p>
